ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068859 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068859 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068859 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068859 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068859 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068859 #edb/ref 1039 "Evaluating a Three-Component Vector-Valued Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068859 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068859 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "26571ec1-e645-5f07-ba4e-75c22558394a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068999 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068999 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068999 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068999 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068999 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068999 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068999 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068999 #edb/ref 1045 "Defining Vector-Valued Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068999 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186069808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068999 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068999 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "bea02a45-ef63-58e9-9f2c-b150acec23b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069412 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069412 #edb/ref 1069 "q-50359" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069412 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $r(t) = ⟨\\cos^{2} t, \\sin^{2} t⟩$, calculate $r(\\frac{π}{4})$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069412 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2dc454b1-c8a7-57a0-a955-d54808121033" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069808 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069808 #edb/ref 1061 44768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069808 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1f95d8fa-a5ab-52bb-954e-880438fde745" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069808 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186070009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069809 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069809 #edb/ref 1061 44769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069809 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4e87da40-1bc7-5937-9854-566b24b722d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069809 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069811 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069811 #edb/ref 1061 44772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069811 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f3b3f68-570e-520e-95c8-902f36d55bbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069811 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186070010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069812 #edb/ref 1061 44773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069812 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2e5ebb70-65ff-51e9-aa1f-59730dada6c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069812 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069813 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069813 #edb/ref 1061 44776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069813 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "063e1808-5b72-584e-a9c3-d3888f153383" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069813 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069815 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069815 #edb/ref 1061 44780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069815 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "677f9f3d-0e2b-5d85-b640-373f0553cba4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069815 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186070011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070009 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we have the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx(t) = t+1, \\qquad y(t) = t^2+2, \\qquad t\\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nThese parametric equations trace out a curve in the $xy$-plane. We can combine these into a single function, called a **vector-valued** function $\\mathbf r (t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r (t) = \\langle x(t), \\: y(t)\\rangle  =  \\langle t+1, \\: t^2+2\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe function $\\mathbf r (t)$ takes a **parameter** $t$ as input and returns a **position vector**. For a given value of $t,$ each value of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ has its tail at the origin and its terminal point at the coordinates \n$(t+1, \\: t^2+2).$\n\n![](images/10/1023d1a1898a402b95d8374f6242d494f714cde9c5ad4cb8186567b9ea192431.png)\n\nEvaluating vector-valued functions is straightforward. Just remember that the output is a vector, not a scalar. \n\nFor the vectors in the graph above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-3) & =⟨(-3)+1,\\,(-3)^{2}+2⟩=⟨-2,\\,11⟩, \\\\ r(0) & =⟨(0)+1,\\,(0)^{2}+2⟩=⟨1,\\,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨(2)+1,\\,(2)^{2}+2⟩=⟨3,\\,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070009 #edb/ref 1065 7721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070009 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5439d77a-b766-5dc3-ad68-6facc632d0f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070009 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070010 #edb/ref 1064 "The **magnitude** of a vector-valued function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left< x(t), \\: y(t) \\right>,\n$$\n\ndenoted $|\\mathbf{r}(t)|,$ is defined as follows:\n\n$$\n|\\mathbf{r}(t)| = \\sqrt{(x(t))^2+(y(t))^2}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude of a three-dimensional vector-valued function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left< x(t), \\: y(t), \\: z(t) \\right>\n$$\n\nis defined similarly:\n\n$$\n|\\mathbf{r}(t)| = \\sqrt{(x(t))^2+(y(t))^2+(z(t))^2}\n$$\n\n**Note:** An alternative notation that's often used for the magnitude of a vector-valued function is $||\\mathbf r(t) ||.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070010 #edb/ref 1065 7722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070010 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1aaa056f-942b-569d-a4db-4051ddf994ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070010 #edb/ref 1067 "The Magnitude of a Vector-Valued Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070011 #edb/ref 1064 "We can also define vector-valued functions with three components. For example,\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf f (t) = x(t) \\, \\mathbf{i} + y(t) \\, \\mathbf{j} + z(t) \\, \\mathbf{k}\n\\end{align*}\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} (t) = t \\ \\mathbf{i} + 2t^2 \\ \\mathbf{j} + 3t^3 \\ \\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nThis particular definition uses the so-called $\\mathbf{i}$-$\\mathbf{j}$-$\\mathbf{k}$ notation.\n\nWe can represent the same function using **angle bracket** notation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f (t) = \\langle t,  \\: 2t^2, \\: 3t^3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nAnother notation that's often convenient is **bracket** notation:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 2{t}^{2} \\\\ 3{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIt's important to realize that all three notations represent the same function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070011 #edb/ref 1065 7723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070011 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "93d7dd93-747b-51cf-b634-a766d1aef404" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070011 #edb/ref 1067 "Three-Component Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275487 #edb/ref 1080 "i+j+k" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275487 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275487 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "017818b3-7eb5-5983-b993-b94ed7bc4f60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275573 #edb/ref 1080 "i-j+k" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275573 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275573 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0baeaf9e-f5d2-51a6-9bd4-e6ff547469a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276262 #edb/ref 1080 "-i-j-k" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276262 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276262 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "57b95886-7b45-5b76-b146-573d86c52385" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276539 #edb/ref 1080 "-i-j+k" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276539 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276539 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7535ebcf-b155-5788-a9e1-0ed34ae0f4eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277537 #edb/ref 1080 "i+j-k" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277537 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277537 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eba0b132-4904-5bf2-8beb-830155e49f3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277833 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186275487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277833 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186275573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277833 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277833 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277833 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186277537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277833 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277833 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277833 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186277537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277833 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "453f511c-3883-5354-9c37-af56c3a1aaec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278334 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278334 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160769" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278334 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278334 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the value of \n $f(s)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡s\\,i+\\operatorname{cot}⁡s\\,j-\\,k$ at $s=\\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278334 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7b01d56e-0cd6-5466-b9de-28d868176a07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278334 #edb/ref 1074 "To evaluate, we substitute at $s=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$  into the function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j-k \\\\  & =1\\,i+1\\,j-k \\\\  & =i+j-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of  $f(s)$ at $s=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$  is $i+j-k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317621 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317621 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317621 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1134010b-c90c-5364-bdbf-6351f4aead82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318092 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318092 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318092 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "99ccd0c5-c9f5-5a83-9bcb-a6eedad3e8a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318461 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186317621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318461 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318461 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318461 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186317621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318461 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "10ddeb3d-5072-5905-b3e1-be70102a5b65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318824 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186318092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318824 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318824 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318824 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186318092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318824 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b8fba009-9b2f-5de7-983c-3162568cd196" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319279 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319279 #edb/ref 1069 "q-348690" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319279 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319279 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $r(t)=⟨cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}),\\,\\sqrt{{t}^{3}-4},\\,ln\\,(2\\,{e}^{t-2}-1)⟩,$ what is $|r(2)|?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$|r(2)|=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319279 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "467bbe03-18b4-513c-ba49-7eedf1cc8a92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319279 #edb/ref 1074 "Evaluating the function at $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}),\\,\\sqrt{{2}^{3}-4},\\,ln\\,(2\\,{e}^{0}-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ),\\,\\sqrt{4},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,2,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the magnitude of this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r(2)| & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319281 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319281 #edb/ref 1069 "q-348693" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319281 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319281 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,\\sqrt{t+3},\\,t-1⟩,$ what is $|r(1)|?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$|r(1)|=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319281 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1bd1a588-edf0-5720-9e2d-306abd5f8af7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319281 #edb/ref 1074 "Evaluating the function at $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ),\\,\\sqrt{4},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,2,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the magnitude of this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r(1)| & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
